---
// Help: claiming authorship (docs/ACCOUNTS.md, "Author verification"; docs/CONTRIBUTIONS.md,
// "Claim").
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const toc = [
  { id: "automatic", text: "When your ORCID iD is on the paper" },
  { id: "claim", text: "When it is not: a claim" },
  { id: "decided", text: "How a claim is decided" },
  { id: "maintainer", text: "Maintainers of the code" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Claiming authorship"
  description={`How an author of a paper becomes its verified author on ${SITE_NAME}: automatically with ORCID, or by a claim the operator reviews.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/claim/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    Being a paper's <strong>verified author</strong> on {SITE_NAME} lets you correct its links and validate its tracing
    map. Most authors become one without asking anything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="automatic">When your ORCID iD is on the paper</h2>
  <p>
    Sign in with ORCID (or link your ORCID identity to your account). Every paper whose published metadata lists your
    ORCID iD among its authors is then yours at once, and appears under "Your papers" on your
    <a href="/account/">account page</a>. The check runs at each sign-in; "Check my papers again" runs it on request —
    after {SITE_NAME} has read a new paper of yours, for instance. It relies on the iDs publishers deposit: an iD is
    trusted only when it is on the paper itself, never guessed from a name.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claim">When it is not: a claim</h2>
  <p>
    If you are an author but the paper's metadata does not carry your iD — many papers list only some of them — open the
    paper's page, signed in, and use "Claim this paper" in its Contribute section: say why you are one of its authors
    (1,000 characters at most, no email address) and, if you can, give a page that shows it (your lab's page, your ORCID
    record listing the paper). The claim waits for the operator; your account page shows it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="decided">How a claim is decided</h2>
  <p>
    The operator reads each claim and accepts it — you then become a verified author of the paper, a role the automatic
    check never takes back — or refuses it, with a few words you read on your account page. Until moderation is part of
    the site, claims are decided by hand, one by one; how they are reviewed is the
    <PageLink href="/policies/moderation/">moderation policy</PageLink>'s subject. At most 20 claims may wait at once for an account, and 10 author claims may be
    sent a day.
  </p>

  <h2 id="maintainer">Maintainers of the code</h2>
  <p>
    Maintaining a paper's code is a different role, proven with the forge rather than the paper: see
    <a href="/help/accounts/#roles">roles</a>. A maintainer may correct what the record says of their repository.
  </p>
</Info>
